A||||7||Spektyr||Off road on the road?||||5||
Z||000000||Spektyr||04-20-2001||05:02 PM||||As a strong believer in the concept of "It isn't illegal if you don't get caught" (aka "If no one saw me do it, I didn't do it.") I have a question regarding the emissions crud on the Fiero.<P>I live in a state that doesn't have any emissions testing, and I'd lay good money that 99% of the cops on the road wouldn't know where to look to find the Cat on a Fiero anyway.<P>So is there any good reason I would want to keep the cat?  (With the exception of the whole tree-hugging clean air thing.)<P>Yes, I realize it isn't legal to drive a Cat-equipped car on the road without the cat.  However, since no one ever checks that stuff around here I'm thinking of the performance benefits of not having it installed.<P>Is there any bad side-effects of bypassing the cat?  Any other reasons I should keep it on the car?<P>------------------<BR>85 GT (to be modified...)||||reg||5
Z||000001||fiero56||04-20-2001||05:10 PM||||I'm wondering what you think the performance benefits are. If you subscribe to that theory, why not disable the EGR system as well.||||reg||1
Z||000002||84Bill||04-20-2001||05:24 PM||||EGR Nooooooooooooooo! the engine will run like krap underperform and knock like hell.<P>As far as the cat well lets just say thats easy to explain to the man. "Honest it think the converter was clogged up so I put this test pipe in" and it sure will sound better without the cat on..||||reg||12
Z||000003||Fierowrecker||04-20-2001||06:02 PM||||Hey Spektyr!<BR>Replace it with a straight pipe or cherry bomb muffler, if you want it quieter...<BR>Damn things are a known problem, and why pay over $100 for a replacement...<BR>The EGR is also a known problem, but too many people are scart to disable them...<BR>NONE of my cars have a working EGR, and they run fine...<BR>crash...<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000004||84Bill||04-20-2001||06:37 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Fierowrecker:<BR><B>The EGR is also a known problem, but too many people are scart to disable them...<BR>NONE of my cars have a working EGR, and they run fine...<BR>crash...</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>What do you run in your cars 100LL av gas? or just retard the timing?||||reg||11
Z||000005||pontiacs4me||04-20-2001||09:25 PM||||I know a guy here in Wichita that had a crack in his egr tube, and instead of pay 80 dollars for a new one, he took the tube off and blocked off the intake and exhaust ports that lead to it, and gas mileage went from 22 mpg to about 14 mpg.  Leave it on!  As for the cat who cares, this is kansas.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Spektyr||04-21-2001||12:48 AM||||I could always do what my grandpa does, too.<P>He takes the cat out, finds a pipe just small enough to fit inside it's inlet, then pounds it straight through the cat.  Reinstall the reamed out cat (with pipe still inside) and Bingo!  Looks stock, but the cat is just an ugly looking straight pipe now.<P>------------------<BR>85 GT (to be modified...)||||reg||1
Z||000007||Songman||04-21-2001||01:50 AM||||I used to remove all the EGR crap on my old hot rods. Not sure about Fieros though. Everyone acts like it has to have them...<P>As far as cats, I live in a city that does require them, but my cars don't. They live out in the suburbs where they are not required. You can tell by looking at the county name listed on the license plate! And none of them have cats.<P>All hot rodders know that you can tune a regular engine to run a lot cleaner than a cat-equipped engine. And most reports show that the fumes put out by cats are more responsible for eating up the ozone than any other car problem. So why do we keep having them? Politics. Money...<P>Some may disagree with me. But I didn't make this up. The facts are out there if you want to look them up. <P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/songman_1.gif"> <BR><A HREF="http://members.home.com/tnfieros" TARGET=_blank>Tennessee Fiero Owners</A>||||reg||1
